EXT. DRY SALT LAKE - HOMESTEAD PORCH - AFTERNOON

A white crust of land that runs to the mountains. A weathered shack with a sagging porch roof. A rain barrel, empty. A windmill creaks, turning once in a while.

An old rancher, JEREMIAH PIKE (70s, suspenders, a face like cracked leather), sits on a rocking chair whittling a stick. A shotgun leans against the rail. He doesn't pick it up.

A rider appears out of the shimmer, leading a skinny horse. The rider dismounts at the edge of the yard: a lean man in a long duster, hat pulled low. A STRANGER.

STRANGER
Afternoon.

JEREMIAH
Afternoon.

STRANGER
I'm looking for water for the horse.

JEREMIAH
Barrel's dry.

STRANGER
So I see.

JEREMIAH
Well's behind the house. Bucket's on the nail. Mind the rope.

The Stranger nods. Leads the horse around the side. A pump handle squeaks. Water splashes. Jeremiah whittles on.

Presently the Stranger returns, hat in hand. He is younger than he looked: forty, if that. A scar runs down one cheek.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
That's a good well.

JEREMIAH
Dug it myself. Nineteen years ago.

STRANGER
Nineteen.

JEREMIAH
You say that like it means something.

STRANGER
Might.

JEREMIAH
Sit if you like.

The Stranger takes the other step of the porch. Lowers himself. Their eyes are on the horizon.

JEREMIAH (CONT'D)
You got a name?

STRANGER
Used to.

JEREMIAH
Used to is a fine name for a man.

The Stranger half smiles. The windmill turns. A hawk circles far off.

STRANGER
Jedediah.

JEREMIAH
(still whittling)
Jedediah what?

JEDEDIAH
Pike.

The knife stops. Jeremiah stares at the stick in his hand as though it had just spoken.

JEREMIAH
Pike's my name.

JEDEDIAH
I know it.

JEREMIAH
I had a boy named Jedediah.

JEDEDIAH
I know.

Neither looks at the other. The wind picks up a veil of white dust and lets it fall again.

JEREMIAH
He ran off when he was twenty-one.

JEDEDIAH
Twenty-one and two months.

JEREMIAH
Took a horse.

JEDEDIAH
This one's his granddaughter.

Jeremiah laughs, short, dry, a sound with no air in it. He lowers the stick. Looks at it. Places it carefully on the rail.

JEREMIAH
You hungry?

JEDEDIAH
I could eat.

JEREMIAH
I've beans.

JEDEDIAH
Beans are good.

JEREMIAH
Beans are what there is.

He rises with a groan. Stops with a hand on the door.

JEREMIAH (CONT'D)
Don't go getting any ideas about staying.

JEDEDIAH
No, sir.

JEREMIAH
I snore.

JEDEDIAH
I remember.

Jeremiah's back is turned. His shoulders tremble once. Then he goes inside. Jedediah sits there a while longer, hat on his knee, looking at the white horizon.

He picks up the stick Jeremiah left on the rail and, with the little knife from his belt, begins to finish the carving.

FADE OUT.
